+      "statement": "For a target discovered at time T=1 deterministically, p_n=P(T=n+1|F_n) equals one at n=0 and zero thereafter. Thus discovery has probability one, while sum_n p_n=1 is finite. This refutes a general necessity claim for the actual stopped target hazard. It does not refute a perimeter-only equivalence specific to critical Boltzmann maps; that would require a model-specific argument. Scope: A generic counterexample only. It supplies no critical weight sequence whose map is or is not exhausted.",
+      "proof_text": "Author-checked proof; independent audit pending. Take any probability space with the trivial filtration and T=1. The event {T=1} is certain and all other {T=n+1} events are empty. Taking conditional probabilities gives p_0=1 and p_n=0 for n>=1. Summing proves the assertion. A conditional Borel-Cantelli sufficiency statement cannot supply the converse from this example.",
