+      "key": "annular-skoro-distance-expansion",
+      "namespace": "probability.reflected-brownian-motion",
+      "logical_name": "annular-skoro-distance-expansion",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Exact finite-time expansion in the annular Skorokhod problem",
+      "statement": "For the smooth bounded connected annulus D={x in R^2:1<|x|<3}, normal reflection driven by w(t)=(-t,0) can increase the distance of two synchronously driven paths. Start X(0)=(1,0) and Y(0)=(cos(theta),sin(theta)), 0<theta<pi/2. Until t<log(cot(theta/2)), X(t)=(1,0) and Y(t)=(cos(phi(t)),sin(phi(t))) with phi(t)=2 arctan(exp(t)tan(theta/2)); the squared distance has derivative 2 sin(phi(t))^2>0. Scope: Finite-time deterministic expansion does not establish positive long-time limsup for reflected Brownian motion. A Brownian support argument and recurrence of expansion episodes would be separate steps; neither is claimed here.",
+      "proof_text": "Author-checked proof; independent audit pending. On the inner circle the inward normal of the annulus is the outward radial vector. For X, take reflection regulator L_X(t)=t, so dX=(-1,0)dt+(1,0)dL_X=0. For Y, choose dL_Y=cos(phi)dt, which is nonnegative while phi<pi/2. The Skorokhod equation then gives Y'=(-1,0)+cos(phi)(cos(phi),sin(phi))=(-sin(phi)^2,cos(phi)sin(phi)). This equals (-sin(phi),cos(phi))*phi' when phi'=sin(phi), and the displayed solution solves that ODE. Both regulators increase only on the inner boundary, both paths stay in the closed annulus, and neither reaches the outer boundary. Their squared distance is 2(1-cos(phi)), with derivative 2 sin(phi)^2. This is a complete deterministic calculation.",
