+      "key": "critical-immigration-logarithmic-lower-bound",
+      "namespace": "probability.spatial-growth",
+      "logical_name": "critical-immigration-logarithmic-lower-bound",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Logarithmic city-count lower bound under the earlier founding-rate estimate",
+      "statement": "Let (M_t) be an adapted nondecreasing integer process, t>=1, with M_1>=1, whose increments are zero or one. Suppose P(M_(t+1)=M_t+1|F_t)>=1/[2(1+K*t*M_t^d)] for constants K>0,d>0. Then constants kappa,c>0 exist such that, for all large integer t, P(M_t<floor(kappa*(log t)^(1/(d+1))))<=exp(-c*(log t)^(1/(d+1))). Moreover almost surely liminf M_t/(log t)^(1/(d+1))>0. Under the earlier city-model founding estimate at alpha=1 and beta>2, d=beta/2-1, giving exponent 2/beta. Scope: The process lemma is proved. Its application to the spatial immigration model is conditional on checking the earlier disk-area bound against the primary model formula. This does not give the matching upper bound or comparable city populations.",
+      "proof_text": "Author-checked proof; independent audit pending. Set m=floor(kappa*(log t)^(1/(d+1)))>=1. Before M reaches m, the founding chance at step s is at least a_s=1/[2(1+K*s*m^d)]>=1/[2(1+K)*s*m^d] for s>=1. Using independent uniforms to realize the adapted Bernoulli increments, compare with independent Bernoulli(a_s) variables until the threshold; after the threshold extend the comparison success variables arbitrarily. If M_t<m then the threshold was never reached, so the sum B of comparison successes from s=1 to t-1 is smaller than m. Its mean mu is at least H_(t-1)/[2(1+K)*m^d]. Choose kappa so kappa^(d+1)<=1/[16(1+K)]. For large t the harmonic sum is at least (log t)/2, implying mu>=4m and mu is bounded below by a positive multiple of (log t)^(1/(d+1)). The Chernoff lower-tail bound P(B<=mu/2)<=exp(-mu/8) proves the probability assertion. For the almost sure statement use t_j=ceil(exp(j^(d+1))) and sum the bounds exp(-c*j). Borel-Cantelli gives the lower bound eventually on these times. Monotonicity of M_t and log(t_(j+1))/log(t_j)->1 interpolate it to all large t.",
